WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:04.280
All right.

00:04.280 --> 00:06.760
So now what is exactly exploring the measures?

00:06.760 --> 00:12.600
What we're going to do is to calculate and find out the key metrics of our business, the big numbers,

00:12.840 --> 00:15.640
the highest level of aggregations of our data.

00:15.640 --> 00:17.520
And the formula for that is very simple.

00:17.520 --> 00:23.840
We're going to go and use the aggregate functions in SQL, like the sum average count for any measure

00:23.840 --> 00:25.040
inside our datasets.

00:25.080 --> 00:30.560
So for example we're going to find the total sales by summarizing the sales value, finding the average

00:30.560 --> 00:36.360
price, finding the sum of quantity in order to have a big number about all sold items.

00:36.360 --> 00:39.000
So always an aggregate function together with a measure.

00:39.000 --> 00:43.360
So for example, if you have a column where you have a lot of values and you go and summarize all those

00:43.360 --> 00:46.080
values, you will get for example 240.

00:46.120 --> 00:47.760
So this is a key metric.

00:47.800 --> 00:50.120
This is the highest level of aggregations.

00:50.120 --> 00:52.320
And the value is not splitted at all.

00:52.320 --> 00:55.880
So for example we say this is the total revenue of our business.

00:55.880 --> 00:58.840
And this is exactly what we mean by exploring the measures.

00:58.840 --> 01:00.400
We will get those big numbers.

01:00.400 --> 01:05.060
So now let's go and apply those aggregate functions to the measures that we have inside our data sets.

01:05.100 --> 01:05.420
Okay.

01:05.420 --> 01:10.540
So now we're going to go and spotlight on the big numbers that matters the most of our business.

01:10.580 --> 01:15.100
So now based on those three tables I have collected here the following questions.

01:15.100 --> 01:16.980
So let's go and solve them one by one.

01:17.020 --> 01:19.500
The first one is find the total sales.

01:19.500 --> 01:28.260
So we're going to go and summarize by using the sum function for the sales amount as total sales from

01:28.300 --> 01:30.220
our table fact sales.

01:30.420 --> 01:32.620
So let's go and execute it.

01:32.620 --> 01:35.740
So this is the total amount of sales in our business.

01:35.740 --> 01:38.020
It is around 29 million.

01:38.020 --> 01:40.620
So this is the business total revenue.

01:40.660 --> 01:42.740
Now we can go to the second one.

01:42.740 --> 01:45.340
It says show how many items are sold.

01:45.340 --> 01:50.180
So this time we need another column but from the same table from the fact sales.

01:50.180 --> 01:52.300
So the question is how many items.

01:52.460 --> 01:56.540
That means we want the quantity and we're going to stay with the same function.

01:56.540 --> 02:02.300
So we are summarizing all the values of the quantity and we can call it total quantity.

02:02.340 --> 02:04.420
Let's go and explore that.

02:04.420 --> 02:10.930
So we can see our business did sold around 60,000 Thousand items, and these 60,000 items did generate

02:10.930 --> 02:12.250
around 30 million.

02:12.290 --> 02:13.410
So let's keep going.

02:13.450 --> 02:16.530
The next question find the average selling price.

02:16.530 --> 02:18.450
So that means you are targeting the same table.

02:18.490 --> 02:20.530
And here we have the price information.

02:20.530 --> 02:22.330
So we're going to say the price.

02:22.330 --> 02:24.570
This time the aggregate function is going to be the average.

02:24.610 --> 02:27.090
And we're going to call it average price.

02:27.090 --> 02:28.890
So let's go and execute it.

02:28.890 --> 02:32.370
So the average price in our business is 486.

02:32.370 --> 02:36.010
So that means our business is selling like expensive items.

02:36.050 --> 02:37.690
Now let's go to the next question.

02:37.690 --> 02:40.610
It says find the total number of orders.

02:40.650 --> 02:43.690
And for that we're going to go and use the function count.

02:43.690 --> 02:46.290
And we can count the order numbers.

02:46.290 --> 02:50.210
So order number total orders.

02:50.250 --> 02:52.010
Let's go and execute it.

02:52.010 --> 02:54.730
So it says we have 60,000 orders.

02:54.730 --> 03:00.770
And now as you are working with the count function, what I usually do I try to count the same thing

03:00.970 --> 03:02.570
but using a distinct.

03:02.610 --> 03:04.770
So distinct order number.

03:04.770 --> 03:10.810
So what I'm trying to do here is first eliminate any duplicates in the order number and then count it.

03:10.810 --> 03:17.830
I don't want to count the same order twice inside our sales, so let's go and execute that.

03:17.870 --> 03:23.190
Now, as you can see, we have only 27,000 orders out of 60,000.

03:23.230 --> 03:27.310
So that means the same order is repeating in our database.

03:27.350 --> 03:28.750
Let's have actually a look.

03:28.750 --> 03:33.630
So select star from our table and let's go and have a look.

03:33.670 --> 03:39.110
Now as you can see from the first order over here you can see the same order is repeated three times.

03:39.110 --> 03:44.150
And that's because this customer did order three things in the same order.

03:44.150 --> 03:46.750
So now of course what is the definition of order?

03:46.790 --> 03:48.750
Usually the whole thing is one order.

03:48.750 --> 03:54.590
That's why in order to get an accurate number of orders, you have to go and use a distinct in order

03:54.590 --> 03:58.950
to eliminate first all duplicates and then count how many orders we have.

03:58.950 --> 04:04.270
So in this scenario, I'm going to say in our business we have around 27,000 orders.

04:04.270 --> 04:07.070
So that's why it is a little bit tricky using the count function.

04:07.070 --> 04:10.990
Always try to compare the numbers before and after using Distincts.

04:10.990 --> 04:13.150
So let's keep going to the next one.

04:13.150 --> 04:15.590
It says find the total number of products.

04:15.620 --> 04:16.740
So it is very simple.

04:16.740 --> 04:19.460
We can say select count and we can say.

04:19.500 --> 04:26.140
Product key as total products from the table goes products.

04:26.140 --> 04:28.220
So let's go and execute it.

04:28.220 --> 04:30.780
So as you can see we have 295.

04:30.780 --> 04:36.540
And if you go and make a distinct just to check you will get the same number.

04:36.540 --> 04:38.180
So that means there is no duplicates.

04:38.180 --> 04:41.140
And of course you can go and count the product name instead.

04:41.140 --> 04:42.700
The names of the product is unique.

04:42.700 --> 04:45.940
So that's why we are as well getting the same numbers.

04:45.940 --> 04:46.900
So that's it.

04:46.940 --> 04:50.300
Let's continue find the total number of customers.

04:50.460 --> 04:51.660
So the same thing.

04:51.700 --> 04:58.500
Select count and you can go with the customer key for example from Gold Dimension customers.

04:58.540 --> 05:02.100
And I'm going to call it as total customers.

05:02.100 --> 05:03.460
So let's go and execute it.

05:03.460 --> 05:07.620
So we can see in our system we have 18,000 registered customers.

05:07.660 --> 05:12.900
Now the next one it says find the total number of customers that has placed an order.

05:12.900 --> 05:18.660
So that means having a customer inside our database doesn't mean that this customer did already placed

05:18.660 --> 05:19.140
an order.

05:19.240 --> 05:23.000
Maybe we have a customer that just registered and didn't order anything.

05:23.000 --> 05:27.600
So what we're going to do, we're going to take the same query, but instead of targeting the customers

05:27.600 --> 05:31.000
table, we're going to target our fact the sales.

05:31.040 --> 05:32.640
So let's go and execute it.

05:32.640 --> 05:34.960
So now as you can see we are getting 16,000.

05:35.000 --> 05:39.520
Which makes no sense because one customer might order multiple stuff.

05:39.520 --> 05:43.800
So what we're going to do we're going to say distinct and let's query it again.

05:43.800 --> 05:44.920
So now it is more correct.

05:44.920 --> 05:47.680
We are getting around 18,000 customers.

05:47.720 --> 05:50.120
Now we can go and compare them one by one.

05:50.120 --> 05:52.280
So as you can see we are getting the same numbers.

05:52.280 --> 05:58.400
So that means all our registered customers did already place an order because their numbers are matching.

05:58.400 --> 05:59.360
So it is very simple.

05:59.360 --> 06:01.480
We are just using an aggregate functions.

06:01.480 --> 06:03.920
And with that we are getting those key values.

06:03.920 --> 06:09.680
But what I usually do is that I collect all those measures in one query in order to have an overview

06:09.680 --> 06:12.120
of all key numbers in our business.

06:12.120 --> 06:17.000
So instead of me querying each one of them individually, I combine them in one go.

06:17.040 --> 06:22.000
So now what we're going to do, we're going to generate a report that shows all key metrics of our business.

06:22.000 --> 06:23.110
So how I usually do it.

06:23.110 --> 06:26.830
I'm going to go and get the first query for the total sales and put it over here.

06:26.830 --> 06:28.990
And now I'm going to build only two columns.

06:28.990 --> 06:33.710
The first one is the name of the measure, and the second one is the value of the measure.

06:33.710 --> 06:35.150
So let me show you what I mean.

06:35.150 --> 06:38.150
Now this one over here, I will not call it total sales.

06:38.150 --> 06:39.790
I'm going to make it like generic.

06:39.790 --> 06:42.070
So I'm going to say measure value.

06:42.230 --> 06:48.630
And before it we're going to make another column from static string value is the total sales.

06:48.630 --> 06:51.710
And we're going to call it measure name like this.

06:51.710 --> 06:54.390
So let's go and just execute this one over here.

06:54.390 --> 06:56.670
So the measure is total sales.

06:56.670 --> 06:59.190
So it is not anymore like the column name.

06:59.190 --> 07:00.990
It is now a value in the output.

07:01.030 --> 07:03.950
And the measure value is like around 30 millions.

07:03.990 --> 07:07.870
Now what I'm going to do I'm going to go and add another measure as a second row.

07:07.870 --> 07:13.550
And in order to do that we're going to use the union all and then copy the whole thing over here and

07:13.550 --> 07:15.390
say total quantity.

07:15.430 --> 07:18.470
And we can change the measure to quantity.

07:18.470 --> 07:21.350
So now let's select both of them and query.

07:21.390 --> 07:25.030
And now as you can see we have now the two big numbers in one query.

07:25.030 --> 07:27.490
So the total sales and the total quantity.

07:27.490 --> 07:33.250
So now what we can do, we can go and collect all those big numbers and measures and put it in one query

07:33.250 --> 07:37.050
so that we have the average price, the total number of orders, product, customers.

07:37.050 --> 07:43.450
And as well you can go and target different tables because SQL cares here only about the number of columns.

07:43.450 --> 07:45.650
And the data type of columns must be matching.

07:45.650 --> 07:48.570
So now let's go and query this.

07:48.570 --> 07:54.490
And now in single query we can see the big numbers the key metrics of our business.

07:54.490 --> 07:57.890
We can see the total sales total quantity average price and so on.

07:57.930 --> 08:03.530
This is a super report where you can generate it for any business where you have in one go the full

08:03.570 --> 08:05.210
big picture about the business.

08:05.210 --> 08:06.810
So this is how I generally do.

08:06.810 --> 08:12.890
If I'm exploring a new database, I put all those big numbers and measures in one query to have better

08:12.890 --> 08:14.850
understanding about the business.

08:14.890 --> 08:15.610
All right, my friends.

08:15.610 --> 08:21.210
So with that, we have now a clear understanding about the dimensions and as well the measures of our

08:21.210 --> 08:21.970
data sets.

08:22.010 --> 08:26.410
Now in the next step we're going to go and start combining stuff together in order to generate insights.

08:26.410 --> 08:31.170
And we can focus now in a very basic analysis it is the magnitude analysis.
